From: programista-zacny <5969291+programista-zacny@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] binder: BINDER_FREEZE and BINDER_GET_FROZEN_INFO ioctls, with the freeze race fix

[combined set, 2026-11-06] This file is three consecutive patches of
staging/backport-freezer folded into one. The diff below is the sum of
the three, unchanged; their headers follow in their original order.

Why folded: scripts/prepare-tree.sh decides whether a patch is already in
the tree with "git apply --reverse --check" on that patch alone. The
second and third patch rewrite lines the first one adds (and the third
lines of the second), so on a tree with all three the reverse check of
the first two fails, prepare-tree.sh tries to apply them again, fails,
and every build after the first stops there. As one file the reverse
check passes (checked against the tree with the whole combined series).
The three separate files remain in staging/backport-freezer/patches/kernel
(0020, 0021, 0022).

======== 1/3: "binder: BINDER_FREEZE ioctl" (staging/backport-freezer 0020) ========

Android's cached-app freezer stops a background process with the cgroup
freezer. A frozen process cannot serve binder transactions, so
system_server tells the binder driver before it freezes a process, and
again when it thaws it:

  struct binder_freeze_info info = { .pid = pid, .enable = 1,
                                     .timeout_ms = 0 };
  ioctl(binder_fd, BINDER_FREEZE, &info);

While a process is marked frozen, a synchronous transaction sent to it
fails at once with the new return code BR_FROZEN_REPLY instead of
waiting for a process that cannot answer; libbinder turns that into
FAILED_TRANSACTION for the caller. One-way transactions are still
queued and are delivered after the thaw. With enable = 1 the ioctl
waits up to timeout_ms for the transactions the target has not finished
yet (new counter binder_proc.outstanding_txns) and fails with -EAGAIN,
leaving the process unfrozen, when some remain; the framework retries
later. The ioctl acts on every binder_proc of the pid, so the
/dev/binder, /dev/hwbinder and /dev/vndbinder contexts of a process are
frozen together.

All three members of struct binder_freeze_info are __u32, so the layout
(12 bytes) and the ioctl number (0x400c620e) are the same for the 32 bit
kernel and userspace of this device as for 64 bit ones. Android 17
already issues this ioctl on this device, when it force-stops an app,
and gets -EINVAL (boot-5 log: "binder: 4883:4939 ioctl 400c620e
6d882d70 returned -22").

Derived from upstream:
  432ff1e91694 "binder: BINDER_FREEZE ioctl"
  005169157448 "binder: fix the missing BR_FROZEN_REPLY in
                binder_return_strings"

Adapted for this tree:
- struct binder_proc and struct binder_stats are in binder.c here; there
  is no binder_internal.h.
- binder_proc_transaction() reads proc->is_frozen once, under the inner
  lock, and uses that value for the return code. Upstream read it a
  second time after dropping the lock, until 0567461a7a6e "binder:
  return pending info for frozen async txns" changed it to do the same.
- The rest follows upstream line for line; the functions touched have
  the same structure here as in 5.12 (inner/outer/node locks, tmp_ref on
  proc and thread, binder_cleanup_transaction()). The places that free a
  queued or in-progress transaction are the same as upstream's, less the
  "fd fixups failed" one this tree does not have:
  binder_free_transaction() drops the count while t->to_proc is set, and
  binder_thread_release() drops it itself where it clears t->to_proc.

Not included, because the freezer does not need them: binderfs, the
freeze notification commands (BC_REQUEST_FREEZE_NOTIFICATION,
BR_FROZEN_BINDER; libbinder only uses them when
/dev/binderfs/features/freeze_notification exists),
BR_TRANSACTION_PENDING_FROZEN, TF_UPDATE_TXN and one-way spam detection
(BINDER_ENABLE_ONEWAY_SPAM_DETECTION, BR_ONEWAY_SPAM_SUSPECT).

Compile-tested (GCC 4.9, lineage_gts210ltexx_defconfig); not run on the
device.

======== 2/3: "binder: BINDER_GET_FROZEN_INFO ioctl" (staging/backport-freezer 0021) ========

Record whether a process received transactions while its binder
interface was frozen, and let userspace read that:

  struct binder_frozen_status_info info = { .pid = pid };
  ioctl(binder_fd, BINDER_GET_FROZEN_INFO, &info);

sync_recv is set when a synchronous transaction was rejected with
BR_FROZEN_REPLY, async_recv when a one-way transaction was queued. Both
are cleared by BINDER_FREEZE, in either direction. The ioctl fails with
-EINVAL when no process with that pid has the binder driver open.

Android's system_server uses the ioctl in three places: on itself at
boot, as the test for whether the kernel supports the app freezer at
all (the freezer stays off when it fails); right after freezing a
process; and before thawing one, where a process that missed a
synchronous call is killed instead of thawed, because its caller already
got an error.

struct binder_frozen_status_info is three __u32 (12 bytes) and the ioctl
number is 0xc00c620f on 32 and 64 bit alike.

Derived from upstream:
  ae28c1be1e54 "binder: BINDER_GET_FROZEN_INFO ioctl"

Adapted for this tree: the new members go into struct binder_proc in
binder.c (no binder_internal.h), and binder_proc_transaction() uses the
value of proc->is_frozen it has already read under the inner lock.
Otherwise as upstream.

Compile-tested (GCC 4.9, lineage_gts210ltexx_defconfig); not run on the
device.

======== 3/3: "binder: fix freeze race" (staging/backport-freezer 0022) ========

Freezing an app takes two steps that are not atomic: BINDER_FREEZE on
its binder interface, then the cgroup freezer on its threads. In
between, a thread of the app can still start a synchronous call to
another process. Its reply then arrives at a frozen process and, with
the previous two patches, was refused with BR_FROZEN_REPLY: the callee
had done the work but the frozen caller saw the call fail.

- A reply is delivered to a frozen process again. Nobody waits for an
  answer to a reply, so it can sit in the thread's queue until the thaw.
- BINDER_FREEZE also fails with -EAGAIN when a thread of the target is
  still inside a transaction (waiting for a reply or serving a call),
  not only when unfinished incoming transactions are queued.
- BINDER_GET_FROZEN_INFO reports the same condition in bit 1 of
  sync_recv, so that userspace can check again after it has frozen the
  threads and roll the freeze back. Bit 0 keeps its meaning. Android's
  CachedAppOptimizer does exactly that ("new pending txns") and
  reschedules the freeze.

Derived from upstream:
  b564171ade70 "binder: fix freeze race"

Applies as upstream; the comment for sync_recv is in binder.c because
this tree has no binder_internal.h.

Compile-tested (GCC 4.9, lineage_gts210ltexx_defconfig); not run on the
device.
---
 drivers/android/binder.c            | 242 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 include/uapi/linux/android/binder.h |  27 +++++
 2 files changed, 258 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/android/binder.c b/drivers/android/binder.c
index d53b0347..47c0cba4 100644
--- a/drivers/android/binder.c
+++ b/drivers/android/binder.c
